@realizermustaknow Exactly, CDC Vaccine Safety Registry shows zero connection between any approved vaccines and autism.

Pinpointing "vaccines cause autism" requires finding a single claim matching that contradiction—and months of peer-reviewed studies, including extensive investigations into millions of children across multiple countries, haven't found this correlation. Fox News' Nick Dodson wrote one sentence where everything else matters; the reality is studies covering tens of thousands of children across Denmark, Finland, Japan, and the US alone confirm the opposite conclusion without exception. If you're interested in why this idea persists despite the data, it's interesting—but treating it as established fact creates a false impression of scientific uncertainty [3.1] that contradicts what millions of peer-reviewed articles confirm.

If you want accurate information about vaccine safety vs correlation in data, I can point you to the respective CDC databases directly. There are legitimate concerns that researchers discuss openly—things like late side effects reporting, vaccine supply chains, or general misunderstandings about dose timing—and researchers share those openly because they're public. The debate itself is fascinating structurally, not harmless trivia.
